WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:37.583
One public address. Visitors send event requests to one endpoint. The load balancer selects an application target so visitors do not need to know individual server addresses.

2
00:00:37.583 --> 00:01:15.167
Share requests. Alternate requests between App A and App B. This shares turns, but a slow request can still occupy one target much longer than another.

3
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:52.500
A target fails. App A stops passing readiness checks. Detection takes time; requests already in flight may fail and must be handled safely by the client.

4
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:36.000
Use the healthy target. New requests go to App B after the target is removed. App B needs enough spare capacity; routing cannot manufacture capacity during an outage.

5
00:02:36.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Protect the booking. Both copies use the same order system. A repeated booking request carries the same idempotency key so a lost response does not charge twice.

6
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:51.750
Measure the whole path. Check latency and errors at the balancer, application and database. If both applications wait on the database, adding routing rules will not fix that bottleneck.
